<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship (1921)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Soviet–Persian Treaty of 1921</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 26 February 1921 between Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Russia renounced tsarist concessions, debts and privileges and agreed to withdraw its forces from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, ending its support for the Soviet republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. A clause allowed Soviet troops to enter Iran if a third power threatened Russia from Iranian territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-persian-treaty-of-friendship-1921.jpg" alt="Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship (1921)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russo-Persian_Treaty_of_Friendship_(1921).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
